Personal dotfiles: a hand-rolled zsh setup (no oh-my-zsh, no plugin manager),
vim, git config, and an agent-agnostic system for syncing LLM skills to
Claude Code and GitHub Copilot CLI.

## Install

Clone the repo, and in the repo directory download the submodules and
install the dotfiles.

    $ git submodule update --init --recursive
    $ ./install.sh

`install.sh` walks every top-level directory (`zsh/`, `git/`, `vim/`, ...)
and runs its own `install.sh`, if present. Each of those symlinks its config
into place using a shared `install_link` helper: idempotent (a second run is
a clean no-op) and safe (an existing real file/dir at the destination is
backed up with a timestamped suffix before the symlink is created, nothing
is ever silently overwritten). It also detects your package manager (brew,
apt, or dnf) to install missing external dependencies like `fzf`.

`install.sh` is safe to re-run any time, e.g. after pulling changes.

## Tests

    $ make test          # every *_test.sh, one summary
    $ make test PATTERN=ssh
    $ make lint          # shell syntax check, plus shellcheck if installed

`make test` is the contract, not an implementation detail: the Claude Code
hooks and the autonomous issue loop both shell out to it, so anything they
run against needs a `test` target and nothing else.

Tests are plain shell -- no framework (`bats` isn't installed or declared
anywhere here), just the assertion helpers in `test/assert.sh` and a `PASS:`/
`FAIL:` line per check. `test/run.sh` discovers every `*_test.sh` under
`sandbox/test/`, `zsh/modules/test/`, and `agents/test/` rather than listing
them, so a new test file is picked up by dropping it in. Each file runs as its
own subprocess, so shadowed `limactl`/`ssh` stubs and fixtures can't leak
between them, and every file runs even after one fails.

Separately, `dotfiles-test-install` builds a throwaway Docker container and
runs `install.sh` inside it twice against a `$HOME` that only exists in the
container -- never test install.sh against your real home directory.

## zsh

### Module loader

`zsh/zshrc` is the only file symlinked to `~/.zshrc`. It doesn't contain
config directly -- instead it sources, in order:

1. `zsh/modules/*.zsh` -- public shell config (aliases, functions, settings).
2. `zsh/prompt/*.zsh` -- prompt segment functions and the `PROMPT`/`RPROMPT`
   assembly.
3. `local/zsh/*.zsh` -- private/machine-specific config, if present (see
   `local/` below).

To add a new module, drop a `.zsh` file in `zsh/modules/`. It's picked up
automatically on the next shell start (or `reload!`, aliased in
`aliases.zsh`) -- no changes to `zsh/zshrc` needed.

Current modules: `aliases.zsh`, `common-aliases.zsh`, `functions.zsh`,
`settings.zsh` (general shell options), `git.zsh`, `python.zsh`, `aws.zsh`,
`macos.zsh` (Darwin-only helpers), and `fzf.zsh` (key bindings/completion,
via fzf's own `--zsh` integration, no-op if fzf isn't installed). Most of
these were ported from oh-my-zsh plugins, keeping only what this user's
shell history showed was actually used.

### Prompt segments

`zsh/prompt/functions.zsh` defines the segment functions;
`zsh/prompt/settings.zsh` assembles `PROMPT`/`RPROMPT` and their colors.
Segments, each a no-op when not applicable:

- `git_prompt_info` -- current branch + dirty marker.
- `git_prompt_worktree_count` -- worktree count, shown only when a repo has
  more than one.
- `git_prompt_ahead_behind` -- ahead/behind counts vs. the upstream branch.
- `python_prompt_info` -- active virtualenv name, or pyenv version if no
  virtualenv is active.
- `aws_prompt_info` -- `$AWS_PROFILE`, if set.
- `ssh_prompt_info` -- an icon shown when the shell is inside an SSH
  session (`$SSH_CONNECTION`/`$SSH_TTY` set).

## local/ (private/machine-specific config)

`local/` is a gitignored directory at the repo root for config that should
never be committed -- company-specific or single-machine overrides. Nothing
under it is synced anywhere by this repo; it's scoped to keep it out of the
public repo, not to sync it to other machines.

- `local/zsh/*.zsh` -- sourced automatically by `zsh/zshrc`, after the
  public modules. Same drop-in-a-file convention as `zsh/modules/`.
- `local/gitconfig.local` -- included by the installed `~/.gitconfig` (after
  `git/gitconfig.partial`), for overrides like a company-specific
  `user.email`. Included unconditionally -- git silently ignores an
  `include.path` pointing at a nonexistent file, so this takes effect live
  as soon as the file is created, no reinstall needed.
- `local/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` -- private agent skills, same shape
  as `agents/skills/` below.

## Agent skills (`agents/`)

Tool-neutral LLM agent skills, authored once and rendered out to each
supported tool by `bin/dotfiles-sync-agents`.

Each skill is a directory under `agents/skills/<skill-name>/` containing a
`SKILL.md` (YAML frontmatter: `name`, `description`, optional `tags`; a
markdown body as the instruction content) plus any optional supporting
files. `agents/skills/dotfiles-help/` is a minimal example.

Run `dotfiles-sync-agents` (on `PATH` once `~/.zshrc` is linked to this
repo) any time a skill is added or changed. It renders every skill under
`agents/skills/*/SKILL.md` and `local/skills/*/SKILL.md` (private skills
win on a name collision, since they're scanned second) into:

- **Claude Code**: `~/.claude/skills/<skill-name>/`, full-fidelity copy.
- **GitHub Copilot CLI**: `~/.copilot/skills/<skill-name>/` (or
  `$COPILOT_HOME/skills` if set), per Copilot CLI's own documented
  personal-skills format -- nearly identical to the neutral one, except
  Copilot has no `tags` frontmatter field, so that line is dropped cleanly.
  This targets the Copilot CLI specifically, not VS Code's Copilot Chat
  extension, whose personal-instructions mechanism is currently
  undocumented and inconsistent in practice.

`dotfiles-sync-agents` also runs automatically at the end of `install.sh`,
so a fresh install picks up every skill without an extra step.

### Claude-only extension layer

`agents/claude-only/` holds content with no Copilot equivalent -- subagent
definitions (`subagents/<name>.md` -> `~/.claude/agents/<name>.md`) and
Workflow scripts (`workflows/<name>.js` -> `~/.claude/workflows/<name>.js`),
both rendered at full fidelity into Claude's output only. The Copilot
adapter never scans this directory, so it's skipped by construction.

## Sandbox (`sandbox/`)

Spins up a disposable, per-task Lima VM with the task's git worktree mounted
read-write, so an agent (Claude Code or GitHub Copilot CLI) can be given
free rein without touching the host directly or ever seeing git push
credentials.

### Working a feature end to end

The shape of a task: spawn a sandbox, plan inside it with you in the loop, let
the agents implement the plan while you do something else, then review the
result one commit at a time. Steps 3 and 5 are the ones with a human in them --
everything else is mechanical.

**1. Spawn a sandbox, from the repo you want to work on.**

    $ cd ~/code/my-project
    $ pj-sbx-spawn add-csv-export --base python3.12 --no-creds

The task name becomes the branch, the worktree directory, the Lima VM, and the
`ssh` host alias -- one name for the whole task. `--base list` shows the
available provisioning templates; `--base none` is a bare VM. Use `--no-creds`
for anything you plan to leave running unattended (see Containment below), and
add `--ports 8000` if you need to reach a dev server from the host.

First spawn of a repo takes a few minutes while the VM provisions. Later ones
reuse the image.

**2. Go in.**

    $ pj-sbx-ssh add-csv-export

You land in `/workspace`, which is the worktree, mounted read-write and shared
with the host. Your shell is the same zsh as on the host. Or open the same
sandbox in VS Code via Remote-SSH -- `pj-sbx-spawn` already wrote the host
entry, and copied a `.code-workspace` file across if the repo has one.

**3. Plan interactively.** Still inside the VM, run `claude` and work through:

    /pj-clarify        # answers questions until the shape of the work is settled
    /pj-write-prd      # writes issues/prd.md -- confirm the user stories
    /pj-plan-issues    # decomposes it into issues/NNN-slug.md, dependency-ordered

This is the part worth your attention. Everything downstream is only as good as
the acceptance criteria written here, and they're much cheaper to fix now than
after five commits have been built on them. Then sanity-check the result:

    $ pj-issues validate    # sections present, blockers real, no cycles
    $ pj-issues list        # what's ready, what's blocked on what
    $ pj-issues graph       # the same thing as a Mermaid diagram

Plan *inside* the VM so `issues/` is created directly in `/workspace`. It's
gitignored working state, so `git worktree add` does not carry it across from
the host -- if you planned on the host first, respawn with `--with-issues`.

**4. Let it run.** Stay in the VM -- you're already there from step 3:

    $ pj-run-issues --max-issues 1

Start with one issue. Read that commit and its review before trusting the loop
with the rest of the queue; if the acceptance criteria were vague, this is
where you find out. Then let it drain:

    $ pj-run-issues

Per issue it implements test-first via `pj-tdd`, runs `make test`, commits via
`pj-commit`, and reviews the commit with `pj-review` in a separate process. It
stops at the first thing that isn't clean rather than building on top of it --
a red suite, an issue `pj-tdd` left for a human, a missing commit, or a second
`changes-requested`. Logs land in `issues/runs/<timestamp>/`.

The project needs a `make test` target: that's the loop's only real check that
the work works, and the `Stop` hook uses it to refuse to let a session finish
while it's red.

A full queue takes a while, so start it under `tmux` if you don't want to hold
the ssh session open for it.

**4a. Check what the run did, from the host.**

    $ pj-sbx-verify add-csv-export

The loop runs in the VM, but this check can't: the manifest it compares against
lives outside the VM's mounts, and a guest with passwordless sudo is in no
position to audit itself (see Containment). Run it inside the VM and it exits 1
saying it has no manifest, rather than falsely reporting clean.
`pj-sbx-teardown` runs the same check and refuses to tear down on a failure, so
it can't be skipped entirely -- but you want to know before you queue up more
work, not after.

If you'd rather drive the whole thing from the host, `pj-sbx-run` pairs the two
halves in one command:

    $ pj-sbx-run add-csv-export --max-issues 1

It verifies, dispatches that same `pj-run-issues` over ssh, and verifies again
whatever the loop exits with. The tradeoff is the ssh session: lose it and the
loop dies mid-issue, where `pj-run-issues` in the VM under tmux doesn't care.

**5. Review and test by hand.** On the host, where the worktree also lives:

    $ cd ~/.sandbox-worktrees/my-project/worktrees/add-csv-export
    $ git log --oneline master..            # one commit per issue (or main..)
    $ git show <sha>                        # review them one at a time
    $ cat issues/reviews/001-*.md           # what the reviewer found
    $ pj-issues status                      # what's done, what's left

Run the app, click around, do the things automated tests don't cover. If
something needs changing, go back into the sandbox and fix it there, or fix it
on the host -- it's the same worktree either way.

**6. Push, then tear down.**

    $ git push -u origin add-csv-export     # from the host: sandboxes hold no push credentials
    $ pj-sbx-teardown add-csv-export

Teardown verifies the sandbox didn't write anywhere on the host it shouldn't
have, and refuses if it did (see Containment). It deletes the VM but leaves the
branch and worktree, so `pj-sbx-spawn add-csv-export` picks the task back up
later with everything where you left it.

### Commands

- `pj-sbx-spawn <task-name> --base <name> [--ports <list>] [--post-create
  <path-or-command>]` -- creates a new branch + worktree on the host, boots a
  Lima VM with that worktree mounted read-write, and wires up a VS Code
  Remote-SSH-ready `ssh <task-name>` entry. Every VM gets Docker, Node, the
  Claude Code/GitHub Copilot CLIs, `gh`, and git-lfs unconditionally.

  - `--base <name>` is **required** and single-valued: it names one of the
    provisioning templates under `sandbox/templates/` (or
    `local/sandbox/templates/`, which wins on a name collision). `--base none`
    is the bare VM, made an explicit choice rather than a default.
    `--base list` prints the valid names and exits.
  - `--ports <comma-separated-list>` forwards each listed guest port to the
    same host port when it's free, or to the first free port scanning upward
    from it -- so the same project can run in several sandboxes at once
    without their ports colliding. The mapping is printed once the VM is up.
  - `--no-creds` withholds the host's `gh` token and the `~/.config/gh`,
    `~/.aws` and `AWS_PROFILE` passthroughs -- what you want for a VM that
    will run an unattended agent. See Containment below.
  - `--post-create <path-or-command>` runs once per worktree, in the guest,
    with `/workspace` as the working directory, after the guest bootstrap. If
    the value names a file relative to the repo root it's read fresh and run
    as a script; otherwise it runs verbatim as a shell command.
- `pj-sbx-list` -- lists every sandbox this tooling knows about (task
  name, worktree path, running/stopped).
- `pj-sbx-teardown <task-name>` -- stops and deletes the VM. The
  worktree and branch are left untouched, so the same task name can be
  resumed later with another `pj-sbx-spawn`.
- `pj-sbx-ssh <task-name>` -- connects to a running sandbox from a
  terminal, landing in `/workspace` (resolves the same task-name slug
  spawn/teardown use, then hands off to the system `ssh`).
- `pj-sbx-verify <task-name>` -- re-checks that the sandbox hasn't written
  anywhere on the host it shouldn't have. See Containment below.
- `pj-sbx-run <task-name> [pj-run-issues args...]` -- host-side convenience
  wrapper: verifies the sandbox, runs `pj-run-issues` inside it over ssh, and
  verifies again however the loop exits. Equivalent to running `pj-run-issues`
  in the VM yourself and `pj-sbx-verify` on the host afterwards, which is the
  better shape when you're already inside the VM or the run is long enough to
  outlive an ssh session.

### What's inside every sandbox

- The task's worktree, mounted read-write at `/workspace`.
- Claude Code, GitHub CLI, GitHub Copilot CLI, and AWS (`~/.aws` +
  `$AWS_PROFILE`) config mounted read-only -- never git push credentials or
  SSH keys.
- Docker, Node/npm, and the `claude`/`copilot` CLIs, installed
  unconditionally.
- This dotfiles repo itself, mounted read-only and bootstrapped inside the
  VM at spawn time (zsh installed, `install.sh` run, shell set to zsh), so
  the same aliases/functions are available over SSH as on the host.

### Auth inside the sandbox

`gh`, Claude Code, and Copilot CLI normally store their real credentials in
the macOS Keychain, which a Linux VM can't reach -- mounting their config
directories alone isn't enough. `pj-sbx-spawn` works around this:

- **gh / Copilot CLI**: forwards the host's currently-active `gh auth
  token` into the VM as `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` at spawn time. Requires
  `gh auth login` on the host; no extra setup otherwise.
- **Claude Code**: needs a one-time manual step, since there's no
  equivalent "current session" token to forward automatically:

      claude setup-token
      echo '<token from the command above>' > ~/.claude/sandbox-oauth-token
      chmod 600 ~/.claude/sandbox-oauth-token

  Every sandbox from then on picks this up automatically. Note the file is
  read *on the host* at spawn time and forwarded into the VM as an
  environment variable, the same way the `gh` token above is -- `~/.claude`
  is deliberately not mounted into the guest, because almost everything under
  it is runtime-writable and a read-only mount fails with EROFS the moment
  Claude Code runs. Without the token file, Claude Code still works inside
  the VM, just via an interactive login the first time.

### Autonomous implementation loop

How the machinery behind steps 3-5 above actually works.

`pj-issues` makes the issue tree queryable -- `next`, `list`, `graph`,
`validate`, `status`. The dependency graph was always there, as prose in each
issue's `## Blocked by` section with "done" meaning the file had been moved into
`issues/done/`; this executes that same rule instead of asking a person to hold
it in their head. Nothing is cached: the filesystem stays the source of truth.

`pj-run-issues` (run inside the VM, or dispatched into it by `pj-sbx-run`) then
drains the ready queue. Per issue: implement it test-first via `pj-tdd`, run
`make test`, commit it via `pj-commit`, then review it with `pj-review` in a
**separate** `claude -p` process -- a reviewer sharing context with the author
has already been told why every decision was right, and reliably agrees. A
`changes-requested` verdict gets exactly one remediation pass before the loop
stops for a human.

It stops on the first thing that doesn't go cleanly rather than pressing on:
a red suite, an issue `pj-tdd` left for a human, an issue that produced no
commit, a leftover dirty tree, a second `changes-requested`. Everything lands
in `issues/runs/<timestamp>/`.

Two things make this safe enough to leave alone. `make test` is the gate -- the
`Stop` hook refuses to let a session finish while it's red -- and the loop
strips `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN` and every `AWS_*` variable from the
environment it hands its children, even in a VM that was spawned with
credentials. It refuses to run outside a sandbox at all without `--force`.

`issues/` is gitignored working state, so it doesn't travel with
`git worktree add`. Planning inside the VM sidesteps that; if you planned on
the host first, `pj-sbx-spawn --with-issues` copies the tree across.

The pieces are usable on their own: `pj-issues` works in any repo with an
`issues/` tree, and `pj-run-issues` is the loop itself -- `pj-sbx-run` adds
nothing to it but the integrity check on either side, which is host-only
because the guest can't be trusted to audit itself.

### Containment

The VM is the blast radius, and it's worth being precise about what that does
and doesn't mean.

A spawned sandbox can write to exactly two host paths:

- `/workspace` -- the task's worktree. That's the point.
- The spawning repo's **real git directory**, mounted read-write at its
  identical absolute host path. It has to be: a linked worktree's `.git` is a
  file containing `gitdir: <that absolute path>/worktrees/<name>`, so without
  the mount every git command inside `/workspace` fails outright, and it has to
  be writable because commits made in the guest write objects and refs there.

That second one is a host code-execution channel. A file planted in its
`hooks/` runs **on the host, as you**, the next time any host-side git command
touches the repo; `config` can reach the same outcome via `core.hooksPath`, a
`filter.*.clean` command, or an `ext::` remote url. None of those files is
touched by anything a sandbox legitimately does -- committing, branching,
fetching and merging write only to `objects/` and `refs/`.

So `pj-sbx-spawn` records a SHA-256 manifest of exactly those files, on the
host, *before the VM boots*:

    $ pj-sbx-verify <task-name>      # re-check at any time

`pj-sbx-teardown` runs the same check first and **refuses to tear anything
down** if it fails, leaving the VM, its SSH entry and its render artifact
intact so there's still something to inspect (`--force` proceeds once you've
looked). Object and ref growth is deliberately not covered -- that's the work
the sandbox exists to do.

The check lives on the host on purpose. The guest has passwordless `sudo` and
Docker group membership, so any guard placed *inside* the VM can be removed by
whatever is running in it.

For an unattended agent, also withhold the credentials it doesn't need:

    $ pj-sbx-spawn <task-name> --base none --no-creds

That skips forwarding the host's `gh` auth token and drops the `~/.config/gh`,
`~/.aws` and `AWS_PROFILE` passthroughs, leaving the VM able to read and write
`/workspace` and little else. Claude Code's own token is still forwarded --
without it the agent can't run. The reasoning is simply that a credential which
was never forwarded can't be exfiltrated by a prompt injection sitting in the
repo the agent was pointed at.

**Residual risk, stated plainly.** This is a boundary against mistakes,
runaway agents, and prompt injection -- not against an adversary who already
controls what the model emits and is willing to spend effort. The guest is
root in its own VM; the integrity check is detective, not preventive (it tells
you something happened, after it happened); and the network is unrestricted, so
anything the agent can read inside the VM it can also send somewhere. Don't put
a secret in a sandbox you wouldn't be willing to leak.

### Requirements

- macOS (Lima's use here targets this user's Darwin machine).
- `limactl` (installed automatically by `sandbox/install.sh`, via
  Homebrew).
- VS Code with the Remote-SSH extension to connect to a spawned sandbox
  from an IDE.
